+# Failed-case fixture maintenance chronology
+
+These actions only retire this Task5 run's failed fixture tasks; they are never business cancellation/RAW proof. All original failure outputs and before/after records remain.
+
+Round1 enumerated seven exact READY/attempt0 tasks by retained current-run route selectors. Its Java guard compared the entire locked task row, subject/input owner relation, absent RAW and external operations/executions, and terminalized each with exactly1 task row /0 attempt rows. Round1 missed one S3 non-restart case because the original resource JSON incorrectly recorded the concurrently started native managed ID instead of actual S3 backend ID. The discriminating repeat therefore remained contaminated; it is retained as raw-boundaries-restart-fixed.
+
+Round2 enumerated ten exact additional READY/attempt0 tasks. It ran after that test command had returned exit1 and before the parent's stricter receipt-link message. Its original guard selection used exact owned route IDs from bounded diagnostics plus task05 owner validation, not a full original artifact linkage. No claim is made that the additional linkage below preceded mutation. Each UPDATE used one enumerated UUID and exact state; no pool-wide or prefix UPDATE ran. Neither round changed subject/domain/input/file rows, schema or old histories.
+
+The read-only Task05CustodyLink program subsequently joined all17 exact cancelled IDs to their actual persisted PROCESSING_IMPORT HTTP idempotency result/status202/actor/resource, original input/fingerprint, meeting owner and actual completed upload/file chain. All17 joined exactly once. cleanup-resource-linkage-addendum.json records per-case attribution. Older receipts omitted owner; their case attribution is explicitly an inference from original database case-start timestamps, not retroactively recorded direct ownership. Seven task-to-case S3 resource mappings required an additive actual-backend correction. The authoritative task/owner/input/accepted chain is exact; this chronology remains a fixture custody qualification.
+
+Before each mutation, an actual /proc cmdline scan asserted no Task05WorkerProcess process remained. The preceding failed integration JVM had returned (command.json exit1, full XML), and worker-exit.txt receipts record actual Process.destroy/waitFor joins for fresh JVM children. The empty-list guard receipts summarize that actual scan. It was not a forecast. No still-running owned Worker/test JVM was interrupted for maintenance. Later tests now await all accepted tasks before route teardown and receipts include owner, actual backend and accepted task IDs.
+
+Executed source custody: round2 code/commands are immutable within cleanup-round2. The root Task05FixtureMaintenance.java initially failed serialization in read-only plan mode (v1 source and partial JSON preserved), then was corrected before round1. After round1 a read-only diagnose branch was appended without altering its apply branch; no prior mutation is attributed to that later branch. Exact tool-invoked edit/commands and the preserved sources distinguish these versions. No further cleanup expansion is authorized by this document.
+
+Round3 was planned and exact-linked before mutation (cleanup-round3/exact-linked-plan.json): two failed raw-full-boundaries tasks, one READY/attempt0 and one RUNNING/attempt1, matched actual owner/input/subject and retained resource before apply. Before/after, code, commands, HTTP/intake provenance and row counts are separate. Its process-pattern receipt used a wrong helper package (ama.integration instead of actual ama.storage); process-guard-addendum.json preserves this correction without backdating. Actual prior worker-exit.txt joined-PID receipts and completed test-JVM exit independently establish the earlier lifecycle. Later correct process scan found no matching child. Round3 is fixture maintenance only. All subsequent genuine pipeline and restart cases reached their asserted real terminal states before route teardown.
